🔹 1. Performance

How fast the system responds.
Latency (p50 / p95 / p99)
Throughput (requests/sec, events/sec)
Response time SLA
Cold start performance
Batch vs real-time processing

👉 Example:
API response time < 200 ms (p95) for 50k RPS
🔹 2. Scalability
Ability to handle growth.
Horizontal scaling
Vertical scaling
Auto-scaling
Elasticity
Scale up vs scale down
Handling traffic spikes

👉 Example:
System should scale from 10k → 1M users
🔹 3. Availability
How often the system is up.
Uptime % (99.9%, 99.99%, 99.999%)
Single point of failure removal
Failover strategy
Redundancy
Active-Active / Active-Passive
👉 Example:
99.99% availability (≈ 52 min downtime/year)

🔹 4. Reliability
System behaves correctly over time.
Data durability
Message delivery guarantees
At-most-once
At-least-once
Exactly-once
Retry strategy
Idempotency

👉 Example:

No data loss during node failures
🔹 5. Consistency
Data correctness across nodes.
Strong consistency
Eventual consistency
Read-after-write
CAP trade-offs
Stale read tolerance

👉 Example:
Read-after-write consistency for user profile updates
🔹 6. Security
Protection against threats.
Authentication
Authorization (RBAC / ABAC)
Encryption at rest
Encryption in transit (TLS)
Secrets management
Rate limiting
DDoS protection
Audit logs

👉 Example:
All PII encrypted using AES-256
🔹 7. Fault Tolerance
System continues despite failures.
Graceful degradation
Circuit breakers
Bulkheads
Timeouts
Fallbacks
Chaos testing
👉 Example:
If recommendation service fails, show cached data
🔹 8. Maintainability
Ease of modifying the system.
Modular design
Loose coupling
High cohesion
Readable code
Clear APIs
Documentation
👉 Example:
New feature deployable without impacting existing services
🔹 9. Extensibility
Ease of adding new features.
Plugin architecture
Open-Closed principle
Event-driven design
Schema evolution

👉 Example:
New notification channel added without code changes
